-> RC stress fixture (T21): exercises the §4 aliasing cases so the refcount
-> discipline is checked end to end. Runs under both the interpreter (code test)
-> and the native backend (built to exe with CODE_HEAP_REPORT for leak checks).

-> objects + property access (aliasing via extraction)
person = { name = "Ada", age = 36 }
assert person.name = "Ada"
n = person.name
assert n = "Ada"

-> arrays + index access
nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
assert nums[0] = 1
assert nums[4] = 5

-> nested aggregates (recursive ownership)
tree = { left = { v = 1 }, right = { v = 2 }, items = [10, 20, 30] }
assert tree.left.v = 1
assert tree.right.v = 2
assert tree.items[2] = 30

-> dynamic string payloads: concat + interpolation
who = "world"
greeting = "hello " + who
assert greeting = "hello world"
msg = "greeting is: $greeting"
assert msg = "greeting is: hello world"

-> spread (co-owning child payloads, plus a string field)
origin = { x = 1, y = 2, z = 3 }
merged = { ...origin, y = 99, w = "extra" }
assert merged.x = 1
assert merged.y = 99
assert merged.z = 3
assert merged.w = "extra"

-> loop over (borrow elements) + yield (collect into new array)
data = [100, 200, 300]
loop d over data get doubled {
    yield d + d
}
assert doubled[0] = 200
assert doubled[2] = 600

-> per-iteration allocation inside the loop body (the memory-churn case)
loop k over [1, 2, 3] get objs {
    yield { val = k, label = "item" }
}
assert objs[0].val = 1
assert objs[2].val = 3
assert objs[2].label = "item"

-> loop over string elements (aliasing string payloads)
names = ["red", "blue", "green"]
loop nm over names {
    assert nm = "red" or nm = "blue" or nm = "green"
}
